From: Note 5 port maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] ipv6: release blackhole route idev on device teardown

An IPv6 socket can cache a one-shot blackhole route while an XFRM policy
exists without a matching SA. Generic dst teardown moves dst.dev to lo,
but without the IPv6 ifdown callback rt6i_idev still pins the removed
tunnel. On the legacy kernel this blocks RTM_DELLINK, netd and the global
IpSecService lock, preventing subsequent MMS and IMS setup.

Use the existing IPv6 route callback to release that idev reference.
The route remains a discard route; no XFRM policy check is bypassed.
Reproduced with a fixed isolated VTI and a held IPv6 UDP socket: ordinary
and active-SA deletion completes in <0.2 s; missing-SA deletion waits
until the socket closes and reports Usage count = 1.

--- a/net/ipv6/route.c
+++ b/net/ipv6/route.c
@@ -204,6 +204,7 @@
 	.family			=	AF_INET6,
 	.protocol		=	cpu_to_be16(ETH_P_IPV6),
 	.destroy		=	ip6_dst_destroy,
+	.ifdown			=	ip6_dst_ifdown,
 	.check			=	ip6_dst_check,
 	.mtu			=	ip6_blackhole_mtu,
 	.default_advmss		=	ip6_default_advmss,
